# Answer 1 · Women's Health in Pakistan and Improving It Within Available Resources (CSS 2017)
@py question 2017-5

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Part one describes the status with data and causes; part two proposes low-cost, high-impact measures — the key words are "within the available economic resources".
@ask ① WHAT IS the STATUS of WOMEN'S HEALTH in Pakistan?
@ask ② HOW could it be IMPROVED WITHIN the AVAILABLE ECONOMIC RESOURCES?
(1) Introduction: health is well-being, not just the absence of disease; women's health reflects their status
(2) Maternal health: MMR 186 per 100,000; many births without skilled help
(3) Nutrition: about 42% of women anaemic; 40% of children stunted
(4) Reproductive health: fertility 3.6; contraception 34%; early marriage
(5) Other burdens: breast and cervical cancer; mental health; violence
(6) Causes: son preference, mobility limits, low decision power, poverty, low spending, few female staff
(7) Existing programmes: Lady Health Workers, midwives, health card, BISP nutrition
(8) Principle for reform: spend on prevention and primary care, not only hospitals
(9) Low-cost measures: LHWs, family planning, nutrition, skilled birth attendance, telemedicine
(10) Social measures: enforce marriage-age laws, engage men and mothers-in-law, health literacy
(11) Better use of existing money: targeting through BISP, public–private partnership, accountability
(12) Conclusion: most women's deaths and illness are preventable at low cost
:::

## Decoding the question
Two parts. **"What is the status"** asks for a clear picture with **indicators and dates**, and ideally the **causes**. **"How could it be improved within the available economic resources"** is the key limb: the examiner wants **cost-effective** measures that do not depend on large new budgets — prevention, primary care, better targeting and social change. Say so explicitly.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
The World Health Organization defines health as "a state of complete physical, mental and social **well-being** and not merely the absence of disease" (==1946==). **Women's health** covers the whole life cycle — childhood nutrition, adolescence, pregnancy and childbirth, and older age.

#### ii) The argument
Women's health in Pakistan is **poor**, especially in maternal health and nutrition, and it reflects women's **low status**: son preference, early marriage, restricted mobility and little say in decisions. Yet most of this ill-health is **preventable at low cost**. Pakistan can improve women's health substantially **within existing resources** by investing in **prevention and primary care**, using proven programmes better, and changing social practices.

### 2. Maternal health
The **Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey 2019** found a maternal mortality ratio of ==186== deaths per 100,000 live births — far higher in rural areas and in **Balochistan**. Only about **two-thirds** of births were assisted by a skilled provider (PDHS 2017–18), and many rural women still give birth at home with untrained attendants. Most maternal deaths come from **haemorrhage, hypertension, infection, obstructed labour and unsafe abortion** — all preventable or treatable. The Egyptian obstetrician **Mahmoud Fathalla** called maternal death the end of a "road" that begins with neglect of girls long before pregnancy.

### 3. Nutrition
About ==42%== of women of reproductive age are **anaemic**, and about ==40%== of children under five are **stunted** (National Nutrition Survey 2018). In many households women **eat last and least**. An undernourished girl becomes an anaemic mother who has a low-birth-weight baby — an **intergenerational cycle** of malnutrition.

### 4. Reproductive health
The **total fertility rate** was about ==3.6== and only about ==34%== of married women used contraception (PDHS 2017–18); many more want to delay or stop childbearing but lack access. **Early marriage** — the girls' age under the old federal law is still **16** in most provinces — means many adolescent pregnancies, which carry higher risks.

### 5. Other burdens

#### i) Cancers
**Breast cancer** is the most common cancer among Pakistani women and is often diagnosed late; a 2019 CSS paper cited about **13,000** deaths a year. **Cervical cancer** is also common.

#### ii) Mental health
Depression and anxiety are widespread among women, linked to **violence, isolation, forced marriage and poverty**, while services are scarce and stigma high.

#### iii) Violence and disasters
Domestic violence causes injury and trauma; the ==2022== floods left about ==650,000== pregnant women in affected areas (UNFPA).

### 6. Causes

#### i) Social and cultural
**Son preference** means girls may receive less food and care. Many women need **permission** or a male escort to visit a clinic, and decisions about care are often taken by **husbands and mothers-in-law**. Many women will see only a **female doctor**.

#### ii) Economic
**Poverty** and **out-of-pocket** costs for transport and medicines delay care.

#### iii) The health system
Public health spending has long been about **1% of GDP**; rural facilities often lack staff and supplies; **female health workers** are scarce, and many women medical graduates do not practise.

#### iv) Education
Female literacy of about ==53%== (Census 2023) limits knowledge of nutrition, family planning and danger signs in pregnancy.

::: fig tree | The causes of women's poor health.
Poor health
- Social
  - Son preference
  - Early marriage
  - Mobility limits
- Economic
  - Poverty
  - Costs of care
- Health system
  - Low spending
  - Few female staff
