  - Taboos
:::

### 7. Existing programmes
Pakistan already has strong foundations. The **Lady Health Worker Programme** (==1994==) brings family planning, antenatal care and immunisation to homes; **community midwives** attend village births; the **Sehat Sahulat** health card covers hospital care; **BISP** and **Benazir Nashonuma** support poor women's nutrition. Articles **35** and **37(e)** of the Constitution commit the state to protecting mothers and providing maternity benefits. The problem is less the absence of programmes than their **coverage and quality**: many rural areas have no LHW, midwives are too few, and the health card pays for hospital care but does little for the primary care where most deaths could be prevented.

#### An example of what works
The **Lady Health Worker Programme**, launched in ==1994==, shows what low-cost primary care can achieve: each worker serves about 1,000 people from her own home, providing family planning, antenatal advice, immunisation and referral. Independent evaluations linked the programme to higher contraceptive use and immunisation in the areas it covered — at a fraction of the cost of hospital care. Its weakness has been coverage and pay, not its model.

### 8. The principle: prevention and primary care
"Within available resources" means getting **more health for the same money**. The most cost-effective investments in women's health are in **prevention and primary care** — not expensive hospitals. Preventing an anaemic pregnancy or an unwanted birth costs far less than treating an emergency. International evidence consistently ranks family planning, skilled birth attendance, nutrition and community health workers among the best-value health investments in poor countries.

### 9. Low-cost measures

#### i) Expand and support LHWs and midwives
Fill vacant LHW posts, pay them on time and equip them — a cheap, proven way to reach rural women.

#### ii) Family planning
Make contraception available through LHWs, pharmacies and clinics; it is among the most cost-effective health investments.

#### iii) Nutrition
**Iron and folic acid** tablets, fortified flour and oil, and nutrition counselling cost little and cut anaemia.

#### iv) Skilled birth attendance and referral
Train and deploy community midwives; organise **referral** and transport (Rescue 1122) and blood banks for emergencies, so that complications are treated in time.

#### v) Telemedicine and female staff
Link women doctors working from home to rural clinics; offer incentives for women doctors to serve in rural posts.

### 10. Social measures
**Enforce child-marriage laws** and raise the age to 18 everywhere — later marriage means safer pregnancies. **Engage men and mothers-in-law** through LHWs, mosques and media so that women are allowed and encouraged to seek care. Spread **health literacy** — danger signs in pregnancy, breast self-examination, nutrition — through schools and mobile phones.

### 11. Better use of existing money
**Target** programmes to the poorest women using BISP data, so that scarce money reaches those at greatest risk; use **public–private partnerships** to run under-used facilities; and improve **accountability** so that drugs and staff reach rural facilities.

::: fig mapping | Low-cost measures and the problems they address.
@left Measure
@right Problem addressed
Lady Health Workers and midwives => Distance and lack of care
Family planning => High fertility and risky pregnancies
Iron, folic acid and fortification => Anaemia
Referral, transport and blood banks => Deaths in obstetric emergencies
Telemedicine and female staff => Women avoiding male doctors
Marriage-age enforcement => Adolescent pregnancy
Engaging men and mothers-in-law => The delay in deciding to seek care
:::

### 12. Conclusion
In sum, women's health in Pakistan is poor — an MMR of 186, anaemia in two-fifths of women, high fertility and late cancer diagnosis — because of **low status, poverty and a weak health system**. But most women's deaths and illness are **preventable at low cost**. By investing in LHWs, family planning, nutrition, skilled birth attendance and telemedicine, enforcing marriage-age laws and engaging families, Pakistan can make large gains **within its available resources**.

## Why this answer scores
- The **status** is described with **dated indicators** and **causes**.
- The second limb is answered on its own terms — **cost-effective** measures, stated as a principle.
- A **tree** and a **mapping figure** link causes and remedies.
- Existing **programmes** are credited before new measures are proposed.
